1
00:00:00,820 --> 00:00:09,736
Welcome to the Oceania capitals, episode two of four. First, I clear the
chosen names for a fresh regional lesson.

2
00:00:10,396 --> 00:00:18,279
Two atoll capitals in the middle of the Pacific, each a ring of land a few
metres above the sea.

3
00:00:18,947 --> 00:00:20,944
Let's bring Tarawa into view.

4
00:00:24,571 --> 00:00:32,199
Tarawa lies on an atoll just north of the equator in the middle of the
Pacific, in the country of Kiribati.

5
00:00:32,849 --> 00:00:45,980
The battle of Tarawa in 1943 was one of the bloodiest of the Pacific War, and
South Tarawa holds about half of Kiribati's people. Kiribati is the only
country that lies in all four hemispheres.

6
00:00:46,633 --> 00:00:55,631
Remember Tarawa as the only country in all four hemispheres, and the atoll
that cost so much in 1943.

7
00:00:56,309 --> 00:00:59,200
Let's bring Funafuti into view.

8
00:01:01,919 --> 00:01:09,291
Funafuti stands on an atoll in Tuvalu, south of the equator in the central
Pacific, close to the date line.

9
00:01:09,945 --> 00:01:28,665
The atoll's highest point is only about 4.5 metres above the sea, and its
airstrip doubles as the football pitch and as the place where people sleep on
hot nights. Tuvalu's internet domain, .tv, earns the country a large part of
its income.

10
00:01:29,333 --> 00:01:35,347
Remember Funafuti as the airstrip that is also the football pitch, four and a
half metres above the sea.

11
00:01:39,847 --> 00:01:49,657
Tarawa lies just north of the equator and Funafuti just south of it, each the
seat of a country spread over a huge area of ocean.

12
00:01:50,314 --> 00:01:57,686
Remember the only country in all four hemispheres, and the airstrip that
doubles as a football pitch.
